<clickhouse>
  <!--
    Stop the test ClickHouse profiling itself into a memory wall.

    THE FAILURE. `packages/db`'s `src/demo/*.int.test.ts` were refused by the
    server, and the refusal moved with the cap rather than with the data:

      (total) memory limit exceeded: would use 2.61 GiB, current RSS: 2.64 GiB,
      maximum: 2.64 GiB: While executing AggregatingTransform

    THE ROOT CAUSE, and it is not the product's data. Measured on the running
    server: `system.parts` held **4.93 GiB**, of which

      system.trace_log              3.11 GiB   238,326,364 rows
      system.text_log               1.41 GiB    36,421,629 rows
      system.part_log             126.68 MiB     2,275,917 rows
      system.processors_profile_log 109.83 MiB    4,188,755 rows

    while the entire `cockpit` database — everything the suites actually query —
    was **74 KiB across 219 rows**. The failing query is
    `SELECT count() FROM cockpit.<table> FINAL WHERE organization_id = ?` over
    tables of at most 160 rows; it cannot want gigabytes. It was simply the
    query unlucky enough to ask for memory while the server was already near its
    ceiling holding its own diagnostics.

    WHAT DOES NOT FIX IT, tried and reverted: raising the container cap. At
    3000M the query wanted 2.61 GiB of a 2.64 GiB ceiling; at 6000M it wanted
    5.27 GiB of a 5.27 GiB ceiling, failing with resident memory at 2.37 GiB —
    under half the budget. The demand tracked the ceiling both times, so more
    memory only moves the number in the error.

    WHICH PROFILER FILLS `trace_log` — measured, because the obvious answer is
    wrong. An earlier version of this file disabled the QUERY profiler
    (`query_profiler_real_time_period_ns` / `_cpu_time_period_ns`) on the
    assumption that sampled stack traces were the bulk. They are not. Grouping
    the table by `trace_type` on the shared test server:

      Memory        10,653      <- memory profiler
      MemoryPeak    10,550      <- memory profiler
      Real              81      <- query profiler
      CPU               28      <- query profiler

    The query profiler accounts for **0.51%** of the growth; the MEMORY profiler
    accounts for 99.5%. `memory_profiler_step` defaults to 4 MiB, so every
    allocation step of every query writes a trace row. Both profilers are
    PROFILE settings, so both are in scope for this file — which is why all four
    are set below rather than the two that looked obvious.

    SCOPE. Only `docker-compose.test.yml` mounts `clickhouse/users.d`;
    `docker-compose.yml` mounts `clickhouse/config` as `config.d` and no
    `users.d` at all, and every other compose file (phase3, oss, rehearsal,
    demo, hosted, site) is either an overlay that adds no clickhouse volumes or
    has no clickhouse service. So this reaches the disposable test server and
    nothing else — staging, the demo VM and the hosted path keep their profilers.

    BOTH PROFILES, not just `default`. `deckgauge_console.xml` puts the
    query-console login on `readonly_profile`, and a ClickHouse profile does NOT
    inherit `default`, so settings placed only there would leave that user
    profiling normally. The block below merges into the profile that file
    declares rather than redefining it.

    A NEW FILE, not an edit to `default-user.xml`: the image's entrypoint
    REWRITES that file on every container start (see the
    CLICKHOUSE_DEFAULT_ACCESS_MANAGEMENT comment in docker-compose.test.yml), so
    settings placed there are clobbered on restart. Files it does not know about
    survive.

    WHAT THIS STILL DOES NOT DO. It bounds the growth; it does not bound the
    TABLES. 238 million rows is not one test run — `clickhouse_test_data`
    survives every recreate, so it was weeks of accumulation with no retention
    policy at all. Retention has two routes and the tracked one is not the only
    one: `ALTER TABLE system.trace_log MODIFY TTL event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY`
    persists in the volume and needs no mount, while putting it in `config.d`
    makes it reproducible on a fresh stack but means adding a second bind mount
    to `docker-compose.test.yml` — which inherits the stranding hazard CLAUDE.md
    documents for `users.d`. Worth deciding on purpose; not smuggled in here.

    ALSO FOUND, ALSO NOT FIXED HERE: the suites leak access-control objects.
    **1,224 of 1,276** row policies on that server are `iso_org_<uuid>` residue
    from the tenant-isolation probes, alongside 51 roles — and both counts were
    lower when first measured hours earlier, so it is still climbing.
    Server-global, never cleaned up, held in memory. CLAUDE.md notes these
    objects are global and that suites suffix them per worktree; it does not say
    anyone deletes them.
  -->
  <profiles>
    <default>
      <!-- 99.5% of trace_log. Step is bytes-between-samples; 0 disables. -->
      <memory_profiler_step>0</memory_profiler_step>
      <memory_profiler_sample_probability>0</memory_profiler_sample_probability>
      <!-- The remaining 0.51%. -->
      <query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>
      <query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>
    </default>
    <readonly_profile>
      <!-- Declared in deckgauge_console.xml; a profile does not inherit
           `default`, so the console login needs these too. This merges. -->
      <memory_profiler_step>0</memory_profiler_step>
      <memory_profiler_sample_probability>0</memory_profiler_sample_probability>
      <query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>
      <query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>
    </readonly_profile>
  </profiles>
</clickhouse>
